A||||2||la9||Timing Marks||||5||
Z||000000||la9||01-06-2000||02:29 AM||||With #1 TDC, do I line up the mark on the cam gear towards the bottom of the block or towards the top. It shows it at the top in the helms manual but I thought you timing marks always faced each other.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-06-2000||02:52 AM||||V-6 ?  If yes then put #4 tdc and the marks will be facing each other.  The book is correct.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cobra427||01-06-2000||03:22 AM||||Its always best to act in accord with what the service manual says. The pic shows the timing gear(cam) with a mark on the top as being # one cyl.T.D.C. and at the bottom, it shows a #4  cyl. at T.D.C. The key here is to determine the relitive position of the camshaft, because the cam gear has to mate with the dowel on the camshaft and still maintain that north/south position while the #4 position marker, is pointing directly above the mark on the crankshaft.<BR>You will have to make sure that the cam lobes on #1 cyl are facing as tho the valves were both closed. You will see where about the cam, as you line everything up, as indicated in the manual,will line up. If the pic is accurate, the dowel should be about 12:30 in its lineup.  And there you have it. A MACHANICALLY timed engine,  Hope this has helped||||reg||1
